var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Automatic-differentiation-and-Reactant","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"Simulations can be differentiated with ForwardDiff, ReverseDiff, Enzyme and Mooncake, and compiled with Reactant. Every commit is tested against every form of each backend, on the cases in test/ad_and_reactant/test_cases.jl: simulations with an environment, with sampled actions and rewards, with timestep sizes and with gradients of the sizes themselves, without states, with a state changed in place, with a selection of what is saved, with maps of names between the model and the environment, with an environment which ends the simulation, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, and on single observations, and agents simulated with an environment or on single observations, with and without a history, and with a state saved with deepcopy. Code in the package's extensions is differentiated in suites of its own, each with its own environment, beside the other tests of the extension. The one for Turing.jl, in test/extensions/turing/ad, differentiates the log density of Turing.jl models of a session, in its turing_cases.jl: with the agent or the environment starting, with imputed actions, with an environment filling in observations, with observations imputed from an environment's distributions, with a reward that isn't given on every timestep, and with a model which samples within each timestep. That keeps the package's own code tested without Turing.jl, since the backends work without it and with other probabilistic programming languages. Every gradient is compared with finite differences. Zygote isn't supported, because the package uses for loops.\n\nBackend Forms tested\nForwardDiff default, chunk size 1\nReverseDiff default, compiled tape\nEnzyme forward, reverse, and both with runtime activity\nMooncake forward, reverse\nReactant compiled value, and compiled Enzyme reverse gradient, on the cases without a Xoshiro","title":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Known-failures","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"These are bugs in the backends, which the package doesn't work around. Their tests are marked broken in runtests.jl of test/ad_and_reactant/ad and of test/extensions/turing/ad, so that the suites pass while the bug is there, and a fix shows up as an unexpected pass. Last checked with Julia 1.13.3, ReverseDiff 1.18.7, Enzyme 0.13.218, Mooncake 0.5.67, Turing 0.49.0 and DynamicPPL 0.42.14.\n\nBackend When What happens Status\nReverseDiff, with and without a compiled tape prepare_state_for_saving using deepcopy, or deepcopy of any container which holds a tracked number zero gradients, no error fixed on ReverseDiff's master branch on 2026-11-06, not yet released after v1.18.7\nEnzyme reverse, with and without runtime activity simulate when the agent starts, actions and rewards are sampled from one seeded Xoshiro, and the number of timesteps is a constant wrong gradient, no error reproduced without the package, to be reported\nEnzyme forward with runtime activity simulate! of an agent with an environment, when the model's state starts as a constant such as (; expected_reward = 0.5) wrong gradient, no error reproduced without the package, to be reported\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity the same throws a MethodError from create_shadow_ret reproduced without the package, to be reported\nEnzyme, all four forms simulate when an observation is NoObservation on some timesteps and a number on the others, and the observations are saved throws an IllegalTypeAnalysisException to be reported\nEnzyme forward and reverse without runtime activity the log density of any Turing.jl model of a session throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError a limitation Turing.jl documents: use runtime activity\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity a session whose data has two types in a column: an action that is MissingAction on some timesteps, or an observation that is MissingObservation or NoObservation on some throws an EnzymeNonScalarReturnException from Enzyme's rule for building a struct of unknown type to be reported\nEnzyme forward with runtime activity a session whose data has an observation that is MissingObservation or NoObservation on some timesteps wrong gradient, no error to be reported\n\nA state which depends on the parameters from the start isn't affected, and neither is simulate! on single observations. A simulation where an observation isn't made on every timestep is right on every backend when the observations aren't saved. The other forms of Enzyme, and the other backends, are right on all of these cases. ForwardDiff, ReverseDiff and Mooncake are right on every Turing.jl case, and so are both forms of Enzyme with runtime activity on a session where every column of the data has one type.","title":"Known failures"},{"category":"section","location":"ad_and_reactant/#Pitfalls","page":"Automatic differentiation and Reactant","text":"These aren't failures of the package's own code, but a model can run into them.\n\nEnzyme reverse and SVector states: updating an SVector in a state with setindex gives wrong gradients for some sizes, with no error. A Vector changed in place or by broadcasting is right.\nEnzyme reverse with runtime activity and a softmax in one line: a softmax written as exp.(v .- maximum(v)) ./ sum(exp.(v .- maximum(v))) gives wrong gradients, with no error, when it runs in a loop on values that differ between the timesteps. The softmax of LogExpFunctions.jl is right, and so is any softmax which keeps its exponentials in a variable and divides them by their sum. It is reproduced without the package, and to be reported.\nCompiled ReverseDiff tapes: a tape records one path through the code, so a model with a branch on a value gives wrong gradients at points where the other branch is taken, with no error. The tests prepare the tape at a different point to catch this.\nTuring.jl models and Enzyme: Enzyme needs runtime activity to differentiate a Turing.jl model, as Turing's own documentation says; without it, it throws an EnzymeRuntimeActivityError.\nTuring.jl models and Mooncake: Mooncake can't differentiate logjoint(model, values) when the model has latent variables under an indexed prefix, such as the imputed actions of a session or a model which samples within a timestep, because building the variables runs DynamicPPL's warning about growable arrays, and a @warn holds a try. Samplers go through a LogDensityFunction, where the variables exist already, and that is fine. The same try is why catch_invalid_parameters of turing_single_session is off by default, and a compiled ReverseDiff tape doesn't see the error it catches either, so the rejected parameters get a finite log density with no error.\nLatent variables in a session: in DynamicPPL 0.42.14, one latent variable anywhere inside a session, imputed or from a model which samples within a timestep, makes every timestep of the session about 450 ns slower to evaluate, and a Mooncake gradient about 10 µs slower per timestep, whatever the number of latents. A session without them costs about 5 ns per timestep. This is DynamicPPL's cost of looking variables up under a prefix that holds some, not the package's, and is to be raised with the Turing developers.\nReactant: the constructors of Distributions.jl don't accept traced numbers, so a model built on them can't be compiled, and sets reactant = false in its test case. An if on a traced value throws, where ifelse works. A Xoshiro can't run in a compiled function, and the default generator repeats its numbers on every run, so pass a Reactant.ReactantRNG() as a positional argument. Compile times grow with the number of timesteps, because the loop is unrolled, so a few hundred timesteps is the practical limit.","title":"Pitfalls"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Models-from-ReinforcementLearning.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"An agent or a learner from ReinforcementLearning.jl can do the learning inside a cognitive model. The cognitive model keeps their object in its state, gives it what was observed on each timestep, and returns the probabilities of its actions, which a simulation samples from and fitting scores the recorded actions against.\n\nThis page shows two such cognitive models. It doesn't simulate or fit them yet, and its code isn't run when the documentation is built, because ReinforcementLearning.jl is a large package to have in the documentation's environment. It was run by hand with ReinforcementLearningCore.jl 0.15.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Categorical\nusing LogExpFunctions: softmax\nusing ReinforcementLearningBase: ReinforcementLearningBase as RLBase\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: ReinforcementLearningCore as RLCore\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: Agent, QBasedPolicy, TDLearner, TabularQApproximator\nusing ReinforcementLearningCore: EpsilonGreedyExplorer, PreEpisodeStage, PostActStage\nusing ReinforcementLearningTrajectories: Trajectory, CircularArraySARTSTraces\nusing ReinforcementLearningTrajectories: DummySampler, InsertSampleRatioController\n\nBoth models list the observations observation, reward and terminated, which are the names that CommonRLEnvironment gives, so they can be used with an environment from ReinforcementLearning.jl as they are. The observation must be the number of the state the environment is in, since the learners here keep a table of values.\n\nBoth also need the environment to move first on each timestep, with EnvironmentStarts, so that there is an observation to act on. On the first timestep of an episode there is no reward yet: the reward is then NoObservation, and the models check for AbstractNoValue, which covers that.","title":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#A-whole-agent","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"An agent from ReinforcementLearning.jl reads the state, the reward and the end of the episode from an environment object, at the stages of its own run loop. This small environment holds what the cognitive model observed on a timestep, for the agent to read.\n\nstruct ObservedByAgent <: RLBase.AbstractEnv\n    state::Int\n    reward::Float64\n    terminated::Bool\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nRLBase.state(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.state\nRLBase.reward(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.reward\nRLBase.is_terminated(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = rl_env.terminated\nRLBase.action_space(rl_env::ObservedByAgent) = Base.OneTo(rl_env.n_actions)\n\nThe cognitive model creates the agent in its initial state, from its parameters: here a temporal difference learner with a table of action values, which explores with a probability of epsilon.\n\nstruct TemporalDifferenceAgent <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    n_states::Int\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, parameters)\n\n    #The learner, with the learning rate and the discount factor from the parameters\n    rl_approximator = TabularQApproximator(; n_state = model.n_states, n_action = model.n_actions)\n    rl_learner = TDLearner(rl_approximator, :SARS; α = parameters.learning_rate, γ = parameters.discount)\n\n    #The explorer, which picks any action with a probability of epsilon\n    rl_explorer = EpsilonGreedyExplorer(parameters.epsilon; is_break_tie = true)\n\n    #The agent's memory of the last transition, which the learner learns from\n    rl_traces = CircularArraySARTSTraces(; capacity = 1, state = Int64 => (), action = Int64 => (), reward = Float64 => (), terminal = Bool => ())\n    rl_trajectory = Trajectory(rl_traces, DummySampler(), InsertSampleRatioController())\n\n    return (; rl_agent = Agent(QBasedPolicy(rl_learner, rl_explorer), rl_trajectory))\nend\n\nOn each timestep, the cognitive model takes the agent through the stage of its run loop that fits what was observed, and returns the probabilities its policy gives each action. The agent is changed in place, so the same object is passed on as the new state.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, input::ModelInput)\n\n    # Unpack input ##\n    rl_agent = input.previous_state.rl_agent\n    (; observation, reward, terminated) = input.observation\n\n    # Update state ##\n    if reward isa AbstractNoValue\n        #An episode starts, so there is nothing to learn from yet\n        rl_env = ObservedByAgent(observation, 0.0, false, model.n_actions)\n        push!(rl_agent, PreEpisodeStage(), rl_env)\n    else\n        #Give the agent what followed its previous action, and let it learn from it\n        rl_env = ObservedByAgent(observation, reward, terminated, model.n_actions)\n        push!(rl_agent, PostActStage(), rl_env, input.previous_action.move)\n        RLBase.optimise!(rl_agent, PostActStage())\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The policy gives a distribution over the actions, which the action is sampled from\n    Pₜ = RLBase.prob(rl_agent.policy, rl_env)\n\n    return ModelOutput((; move = Pₜ), (; rl_agent))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters = (:learning_rate, :discount, :epsilon),\n        states = :rl_agent,\n        observations = (:observation, :reward, :terminated),\n        actions = :move,\n    )\nend\n\nSince the agent is changed in place, every saved state would be the same object, showing the values after the last timestep. This method saves a copy of the table of action values on each timestep instead.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::TemporalDifferenceAgent, state)\n    return (; action_values = copy(state.rl_agent.policy.learner.approximator.model))\nend","title":"A whole agent"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Only-the-learner","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"A learner can also be used on its own, with one transition at a time, which needs no environment object and no trajectory. The action probabilities are then the cognitive model's own: here a softmax over the learner's action values, with an inverse temperature. The softmax is the one from LogExpFunctions.jl.\n\nstruct TemporalDifferenceLearner <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    n_states::Int\n    n_actions::Int\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, parameters)\n    rl_approximator = TabularQApproximator(; n_state = model.n_states, n_action = model.n_actions)\n    rl_learner = TDLearner(rl_approximator, :SARS; α = parameters.learning_rate, γ = parameters.discount)\n\n    #The learner needs the observation before the action too, so that is kept in the state\n    return (; rl_learner, previous_observation = 0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, input::ModelInput)\n\n    # Unpack input ##\n    (; rl_learner, previous_observation) = input.previous_state\n    (; observation, reward, terminated) = input.observation\n    β = input.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Let the learner learn from what followed the previous action, unless an episode starts\n    if !(reward isa AbstractNoValue)\n        rl_transition = (; state = previous_observation, next_state = observation, action = input.previous_action.move, reward, terminal = terminated)\n        RLBase.optimise!(rl_learner, rl_transition)\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The learner's value of each action where the agent is now\n    Qₜ = RLCore.forward(rl_learner, observation)\n    #Softmax over the action values\n    Pₜ = Categorical(softmax(β .* Qₜ))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; move = Pₜ), (; rl_learner, previous_observation = observation))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters = (:learning_rate, :discount, :inverse_temperature),\n        states = (:rl_learner, :previous_observation),\n        observations = (:observation, :reward, :terminated),\n        actions = :move,\n    )\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::TemporalDifferenceLearner, state)\n    return (; action_values = copy(state.rl_learner.approximator.model), state.previous_observation)\nend","title":"Only the learner"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_reinforcement_learning/#Fitting","page":"Models from ReinforcementLearning.jl as cognitive models","text":"This page doesn't fit the cognitive models yet. When they are fitted with a sampler that uses gradients, use Enzyme.jl in reverse mode with runtime activity as the automatic differentiation backend, which for Turing.jl is AutoEnzyme(; mode = set_runtime_activity(Reverse), function_annotation = Const). It gave the right gradient of the log density of a session for both cognitive models here, when checked against finite differences with Enzyme.jl 0.13. ForwardDiff.jl and ReverseDiff.jl can't differentiate either of them, because the learner keeps its learning rate and its discount in Float64 fields, which the number types of those two don't fit in.\n\nUse the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl, as above, and not one written by hand. Written in one line, a softmax can get a wrong gradient from Enzyme's reverse mode, with no error, which the page Automatic differentiation and Reactant describes under its pitfalls.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/cognitive_abm_with_agents/#Cognitive-agent-based-modelling-with-Agents.jl","page":"Cognitive agent-based modelling with Agents.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to put a CognitiveAgent into an agent-based model from Agents.jl, with what each agent observes in the world and what its actions do to it.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Cognitive agent-based modelling with Agents.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"These are only used inside the package, and can change without notice. What users work with is on the page API.\n\nPages = [\"internals.md\"]","title":"Internals"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel","page":"Internals","text":"AbstractModel\n\nSupertype for cognitive models and environment models, which the functions that treat the two in the same way take. A model is a subtype of AbstractCognitiveModel or of AbstractEnvironmentModel, and not of this type itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.as_tuple_of_names-Tuple{Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Internals","text":"as_tuple_of_names(names)\n\nReturn names as a Tuple of Symbols, which is how a ModelAttributes keeps them: a tuple as it is, a single Symbol in a tuple, and a Vector of them as a tuple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_tuple_of_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.actions_before_first_timestep-Union{Tuple{ModelAttributes{Tparameters, Tstates, Tobservations, Tactions}}, Tuple{Tactions}, Tuple{Tobservations}, Tuple{Tstates}, Tuple{Tparameters}} where {Tparameters, Tstates, Tobservations, Tactions}","page":"Internals","text":"actions_before_first_timestep(attributes)\nobservations_before_first_timestep(attributes)\n\nReturn a NamedTuple with the action or the observation names in attributes, a ModelAttributes, each BeforeFirstTimestep. The names are read from the type, so that the NamedTuple is known at compile time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.actions_before_first_timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_match_attribute_names-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"check_match_attribute_names(model, parameters, state, previous_action)\ncheck_match_attribute_names(environment, parameters, state, previous_observation)\n\nThrow an informative error if the names of the parameters, of the previous action of a cognitive model or the previous observation of an environment model, or of a state that is a NamedTuple, aren't those that attribute_names lists.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_match_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_observation_names-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"check_observation_names(model, observation)\n\nThrow an informative error if observation, given to a cognitive model without an environment, isn't a NamedTuple with every observation that attribute_names lists for model. It can have more than those.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_observation_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_type_attribute_names-Tuple{ModelAttributes, CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"check_type_attribute_names(attributes, model)\n\nReturn attributes if it is the ModelAttributes that a method of attribute_names must return, and throw an informative error otherwise. model is the cognitive model or the environment model whose method returned it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_type_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.has_attribute_names-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"has_attribute_names(model)\n\nReturn whether model, a cognitive model or an environment model, has its own method of attribute_names, instead of one of the two fallbacks which throw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.has_name-Tuple{Symbol, Tuple{}}","page":"Internals","text":"has_name(name, names)\nhas_every_name(names, other_names)\n\nReturn whether name is among the tuple names, and whether every name in the tuple names is among other_names.\n\nBoth are unrolled over the names, so that the compiler decides them from the names alone when it knows them, which it doesn't for issubset and issetequal with more than one name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.has_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initial_action-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"initial_action(model)\ninitial_observation(model)\n\nReturn the action and the observation from before the first timestep: a NamedTuple with the action or the observation names that attribute_names lists for model, a cognitive model or an environment model, each BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initial_action"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.print_attribute_names-Tuple{IO, ModelAttributes}","page":"Internals","text":"print_attribute_names(io, attributes)\n\nPrint a line for each kind of attribute in attributes, a ModelAttributes, with none if it has no names of that kind.\n\nThe show methods of a ModelAttributes, of a cognitive model and of an environment model each print a header of their own, and then these lines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.print_attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.same_names-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"same_names(names, other_names)\n\nReturn whether two tuples of names have the same names, in any order.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.same_names"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_names_fit","page":"Internals","text":"check_names_fit(cognitive_model, environment, observation_map=nothing,\n                action_map=nothing)\n\nThrow an informative error if cognitive_model and environment can't be simulated together. Without a map, they must list the same observations and the same actions in attribute_names. With a NameMap for the observations or the actions, the map must take names the giver lists, and give exactly the names the receiver lists.\n\nThe names are in the types, so the check is decided at compile time and costs nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_names_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_observations_fit-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Nothing}","page":"Internals","text":"check_observations_fit(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_model_names,\n                       environment_names, observation_map)\ncheck_actions_fit(cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_model_names,\n                  environment_names, action_map)\n\nThrow an informative error if the observations environment gives don't fit those cognitive_model lists, or the actions cognitive_model gives don't fit those environment lists, as check_names_fit describes. The methods for a NameMap are with the simulation code, where that type is defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_observations_fit"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.describe_model-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"Internals","text":"describe_model(model)\n\nReturn how model is named in an error message: as a cognitive model or as an environment model, with its type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.describe_model"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.as_selection-Tuple{Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}}","page":"Internals","text":"as_selection(names)\n\nReturn names as a selection, which is how a SaveHistory keeps it: a Tuple of Symbols, made from a single Symbol or a Vector of them too, or SaveEverything as it is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.as_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_savehistory_selection_names-Tuple{SaveHistory, CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel}","page":"Internals","text":"check_savehistory_selection_names(save_history, model)\n\nThrow an informative error if save_history selects an observation or an action by name that attribute_names of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, doesn't list, since a history starts from those names. The states aren't checked, because prepare_state_for_saving can save anything.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_savehistory_selection_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_selected_names-Tuple{SaveEverything, Any, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"check_selected_names(selection, names, kind, model)\n\nThrow an informative error if selection has a name that isn't among names, those that model lists for kind, which is \"observations\" or \"actions\". SaveEverything can always be selected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_selected_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.observation_selection-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{Tobservations, Tactions, Tstates}}, Tuple{Tstates}, Tuple{Tactions}, Tuple{Tobservations}} where {Tobservations, Tactions, Tstates}","page":"Internals","text":"observation_selection(save_history)\naction_selection(save_history)\nstate_selection(save_history)\n\nReturn the selection of one kind in a SaveHistory as a Val, so that the names are known at compile time wherever something is selected with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.observation_selection"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.save_state-Tuple{Any, Any, Val}","page":"Internals","text":"save_state(model, state, selection::Val)\n\nReturn what is saved of state: what selection selects of the output of prepare_state_for_saving. When nothing is selected, the state isn't prepared at all, so that no copy is made of a state which isn't saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.save_state"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saved_timestep_sizes-Tuple{Any, Any, SaveHistory}","page":"Internals","text":"saved_timestep_sizes(timestep_sizes, n_steps, save_history)\n\nReturn the sizes of n_steps timesteps as they are returned with a history: one size for each timestep when save_history saves anything, and an empty vector when it saves nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saved_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.savehistory_states_only-Union{Tuple{SaveHistory{Tobservations, Tactions, Tstates}}, Tuple{Tstates}, Tuple{Tactions}, Tuple{Tobservations}} where {Tobservations, Tactions, Tstates}","page":"Internals","text":"savehistory_states_only(save_history)\n\nReturn a SaveHistory which selects only the states that save_history selects, which is what simulate keeps of an environment's selection.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.savehistory_states_only"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.saves_nothing-Tuple{SaveHistory{(), (), ()}}","page":"Internals","text":"saves_nothing(save_history)\n\nReturn whether save_history selects nothing of any kind, which is known at compile time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.saves_nothing"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.select_names-Tuple{Any, Val{SaveEverything()}}","page":"Internals","text":"select_names(x, selection::Val)\n\nReturn what a SaveHistory selects of x: x itself for SaveEverything, an empty NamedTuple for no names, and the named entries of a NamedTuple for a tuple of names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.select_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.given_name-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"given_name(name)\nreceived_name(name)\n\nReturn the name an output is given under and the name it is received under, from one argument of a NameMap: the two sides of a :given => :received pair, or a single Symbol, which is received under the name it is given with.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.given_name"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.starting_history-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel, Any, Any, SaveHistory}","page":"Internals","text":"starting_history(cognitive_model, cognitive_state, previous_action, save_history)\nstarting_history(environment_model, state, previous_observation, save_history)\n\nReturn the history that an agent or an environment starts with: an AgentHistory or an EnvironmentHistory with what save_history selects of where it starts, and BeforeFirstTimestep as the size of the timestep, since none has run yet. When nothing is selected, the vectors are empty.\n\nAn agent hasn't observed anything when it starts, and an environment hasn't received an action, so that entry has every name the model lists as BeforeFirstTimestep(). reset! starts from the same history as the constructors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.starting_history"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_environment_output-Tuple{EnvironmentOutput, AbstractEnvironmentModel}","page":"Internals","text":"check_environment_output(output, environment)\n\nReturn output if it is the EnvironmentOutput that an apply_model method of environment must return, and throw an error otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_environment_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.check_model_output-Tuple{ModelOutput, AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"Internals","text":"check_model_output(output, cognitive_model)\n\nReturn output if it is the ModelOutput that an apply_model method of cognitive_model must return, and throw an error otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.check_model_output"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.collect_timestep_outputs-NTuple{10, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"collect_timestep_outputs(environment_output, cognitive_output,\n                         observation_from_environment, observation, action,\n                         action_to_environment, cognitive_model, environment,\n                         save_history, save_environment_history)\n\nCollect the outputs of a timestep in a NamedTuple, each both as it is, which the next timestep starts from, and as it is saved, which save_history and save_environment_history select.\n\nobservation_from_environment is the observation sampled from what the environment gave, and observation is the same observation as the cognitive model receives it. action_to_environment is action as the environment receives it. Each pair differs when a NameMap selects or renames them. The cognitive model's history holds the observation as the cognitive model receives it, and the environment's the action as the environment does.\n\nThis is its own function so that if an output can have two types, like an observation with an entry that is sometimes NoObservation, the compiler can split the call and get a concrete type for each.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.collect_timestep_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.each_timestep_size-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"each_timestep_size(timestep_sizes, n_steps)\n\nReturn the size of each of n_steps timesteps as a vector, for a history. A vector of sizes is returned as it is, and anything else is repeated for every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.each_timestep_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.get_timestep_size-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"get_timestep_size(timestep_sizes, t)\n\nReturn the size of timestep t. A vector has one size for each timestep, and anything else is the size of all of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.get_timestep_size"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_storage-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"initialize_storage(first_value, n_steps)\n\nCreate the vector for saving one kind of output, using the first value to set its type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_storage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.map_names-Tuple{Any, Nothing}","page":"Internals","text":"map_names(x, name_map)\n\nReturn what the receiver gets of the outputs x: all of x without a map, and with a NameMap the outputs it takes, under the names it gives them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.map_names"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.merge_default_parameters-Tuple{CognitiveModeling.AbstractModel, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"merge_default_parameters(model, parameters)\n\nReturn parameters with the values from default_parameters of model, a cognitive model or an environment model, for the parameters it doesn't have, if model provides defaults for them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.merge_default_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.push_output!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"push_output!(storage, value)\n\nAdd value at the end of storage, and return the vector. As for store_output!, a value that doesn't fit the type of storage moves the outputs so far to a wider vector, which is returned instead. Starting from Vector{Union{}}() lets the first value set the type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.push_output!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.returned_timestep_sizes-Tuple{AbstractVector, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"returned_timestep_sizes(timestep_sizes, n_timesteps)\n\nReturn the sizes of the n_timesteps timesteps that ran, as a simulation returns them. A vector of sizes is cut to those timesteps, since an environment can end a simulation early, and anything else is returned as it is, since it is the size of every timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.returned_timestep_sizes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.run_remaining_timesteps-NTuple{14, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"run_remaining_timesteps(storage, outputs, who_starts, cognitive_model, environment,\n                        cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters, save_history,\n                        save_environment_history, observation_map, action_map, rng,\n                        timestep_sizes, n_steps)\n\nRun the timesteps after the first, and return the NamedTuple of vectors with the saved outputs of the timesteps that ran, followed by the outputs of the last one. The timesteps end before n_steps if stopping_criterion of environment is met.\n\nThis is its own function so that it's compiled for the types of the first outputs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.run_remaining_timesteps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.run_timesteps-NTuple{13, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"run_timesteps(previous_outputs, who_starts, cognitive_model, environment,\n              cognitive_parameters, environment_parameters, save_history,\n              save_environment_history, observation_map, action_map, rng,\n              timestep_sizes, n_steps)\n\nRun up to n_steps timesteps starting from previous_outputs, and return a NamedTuple of vectors with the saved outputs of the timesteps that ran, followed by the outputs of the last one. The timesteps end before n_steps if stopping_criterion of environment is met, and an environment where it is met already can't run any.\n\nThe vectors are observations, actions, cognitive_states and environment_states, as save_history and save_environment_history select them, and observations_from_environment and actions_to_environment with what save_environment_history selects of the observations the environment gave and the actions it received, for the environment's history.\n\nsimulate! continues from the last outputs instead of from what was saved, because prepare_state_for_saving can return anything, and because the next simulation can change a state in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.run_timesteps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.sample_action-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"sample_action(rng, action_distribution)\n\nSample an action from what a model returned, using rand. Each action in a NamedTuple is sampled on its own, so a NamedTuple inside it gives a NamedTuple of samples, and an entry that is NoAction is kept. A number, a string or a symbol is a value and not something to sample from, and throws an ArgumentError which names Deterministic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sample_action"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.sample_observation-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"sample_observation(rng, observation_distribution)\n\nSample an observation from what an environment model returned, using rand, as sample_action does for an action. Each observation in a NamedTuple is sampled on its own, and an entry that is NoObservation is kept. A number, a string or a symbol throws an ArgumentError here too.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.sample_observation"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.store_output!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Vector{T}, Any, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"store_output!(storage, value, t)\n\nSave value as element t of storage, and return the vector.\n\nIf the value doesn't fit the type of storage, the outputs so far are moved to a wider vector which can hold both types, and that vector is returned instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.store_output!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.timestep-NTuple{8, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"timestep(cognitive_model, observation, previous_cognitive_state, previous_action,\n         cognitive_parameters, timestep_size, rng, save_history)\n\nRun a timestep of cognitive_model on observation, without an environment, and return the action sampled from its output, its new state, and what save_history selects of the observation, the action and the state, in a NamedTuple. The observation must have the names that cognitive_model lists.\n\nsimulate and simulate! on a single observation both run this. It is its own function so that it's compiled for the types in an agent's untyped fields, which makes simulate! on a single observation several times faster, and makes the check of the observation's names cost nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.timestep-Tuple{AgentStarts, Vararg{Any, 11}}","page":"Internals","text":"timestep(::AgentStarts, cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_parameters,\n         environment_parameters, save_history, save_environment_history,\n         observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_size, previous)\n\nRun a timestep where the agent moves first, starting from the outputs of the timestep before, and return its outputs.\n\nThis is always inlined, because a call here makes the loop several times slower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.timestep-Tuple{EnvironmentStarts, Vararg{Any, 11}}","page":"Internals","text":"timestep(::EnvironmentStarts, cognitive_model, environment, cognitive_parameters,\n         environment_parameters, save_history, save_environment_history,\n         observation_map, action_map, rng, timestep_size, previous)\n\nRun a timestep where the environment moves first, starting from the outputs of the timestep before, and return its outputs.\n\nThis is always inlined, because a call here makes the loop several times slower.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.timestep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.is_recorded-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"is_recorded(action)\n\nReturn whether a single action in the data was taken and recorded, so that it is scored: neither MissingAction nor NoAction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.is_recorded"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.recorded_actions-Union{Tuple{NamedTuple{names}}, Tuple{names}} where names","page":"Internals","text":"recorded_actions(actions)\n\nReturn the recorded values of each action in actions, a NamedTuple with one vector per action, as a NamedTuple of vectors in the order of the timesteps, leaving out every action without recorded values. Fitting scores these against the model's action distributions on the same timesteps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.recorded_actions"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.row-Tuple{NamedTuple, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"row(columns, t)\n\nReturn the NamedTuple of the values at timestep t of columns, a NamedTuple of vectors with one entry for each timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.row"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#CognitiveModeling.without_missing_actions-Tuple{NamedTuple}","page":"Internals","text":"without_missing_actions(actions)\n\nReturn actions, a NamedTuple with one vector per action, with every MissingAction replaced by NoAction, for a session where a missing action is passed on as an action that wasn't taken instead of being imputed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.without_missing_actions"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Planned-tutorials-and-interfaces","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"This page lists what is planned and not written yet. The list will grow.","title":"Planned tutorials and interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Cognitive-models-for-tutorials","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"Models of metacognition, such as HMeta-d.\nThe models of hBayesDM, probably every one of them.\nThe main models for the Iowa Gambling Task, such as PVL-Delta and ORL.\nDifferent variants of the Rescorla-Wagner model, and models like it.\nEvidence accumulation models, such as attention-based models.\nBayesian models made with RxInfer.jl.\nActive inference models, which a separate package is being made for.","title":"Cognitive models for tutorials"},{"category":"section","location":"planned/#Interfaces","page":"Planned tutorials and interfaces","text":"BayesianRegressionModels.jl.","title":"Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"The functions and types that are only used inside the package are on the page Internals.\n\nPages = [\"api.md\"]","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Public","page":"API","text":"","title":"Public"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Defining-models-and-environments","page":"API","text":"These are what a model or an environment is written with. They are public, but not exported, so use them with the package's name, as in CognitiveModeling.apply_model.","title":"Defining models and environments"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Functions-that-a-model-must-implement","page":"API","text":"","title":"Functions that a model must implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Functions-that-an-environment-model-must-implement","page":"API","text":"","title":"Functions that an environment model must implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Functions-that-a-model-or-an-environment-model-can-implement","page":"API","text":"","title":"Functions that a model or an environment model can implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Functions-that-an-environment-model-can-implement","page":"API","text":"","title":"Functions that an environment model can implement"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Display","page":"API","text":"","title":"Display"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.CognitiveModeling","page":"API","text":"Computational modeling of cognition and behaviour.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CognitiveModeling"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractCognitiveModel","page":"API","text":"AbstractCognitiveModel\n\nSupertype for cognitive models.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add methods of apply_model, initialize_state and attribute_names for it. A model can also add methods of prepare_state_for_saving, default_parameters and turing_apply_model.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractCognitiveModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractNoValue","page":"API","text":"AbstractNoValue\n\nSupertype for what stands in for an observation or an action that isn't there: NoObservation, NoAction and BeforeFirstTimestep. A model which treats them all the same can check for it, as in input.observation.reward isa AbstractNoValue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractNoValue"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.BeforeFirstTimestep","page":"API","text":"BeforeFirstTimestep()\n\nStand-in for an observation or an action from before the first timestep, when there is none yet, as one entry of a NamedTuple, such as (; choice = BeforeFirstTimestep()).\n\nA cognitive model receives it for every previous action on its first timestep, and for every observation too when the agent starts. An environment model receives it for every previous observation on its first timestep, and for every action too when the environment starts. A history starts with it, also as the size of the timestep. NoObservation and NoAction are different: they say that nothing was observed or done on a timestep that did run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.BeforeFirstTimestep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.Deterministic","page":"API","text":"Deterministic(value)\n\nAn action or an observation that is always value, for a cognitive model or an environment model which gives it without any randomness, as in (; reward = Deterministic(1.0)).\n\nA simulation samples every action and every observation with rand, and sampling from a Deterministic gives value itself. The value is kept in the field value.\n\nA simulation throws an error for a number, a string or a symbol that isn't wrapped, since none of them is something to sample from. A vector, a tuple or a range that isn't wrapped is sampled from as it is, which is rarely what is meant: rand picks one of its elements, with no error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Random: Xoshiro\n\njulia> rand(Xoshiro(1), Deterministic(1.0))\n1.0\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Deterministic(true))).action_distribution.choice.value\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.Deterministic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.InvalidParametersError","page":"API","text":"InvalidParametersError(message)\nInvalidParametersError()\n\nAn error for a model to throw from apply_model or initialize_state when its parameters aren't valid, as in throw(InvalidParametersError()).\n\nThe default message says that the parameters are not valid, possibly dependent on the observations, actions and states, and a model can give a more specific one. A simulation stops with it as with any other error, and fitting can catch it to give the parameters a probability of zero, so a model should throw this rather than an ordinary error when the parameters are the problem.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> try\n           throw(InvalidParametersError())\n       catch error\n           sprint(showerror, error)\n       end\n\"InvalidParametersError: Model parameters are not valid, possibly dependent on observations, actions and states.\"\n\njulia> sprint(showerror, InvalidParametersError(\"The learning rate must be between 0 and 1.\"))\n\"InvalidParametersError: The learning rate must be between 0 and 1.\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.InvalidParametersError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.ModelAttributes","page":"API","text":"ModelAttributes(; parameters, states, observations, actions)\n\nThe names of the attributes of a model, which attribute_names returns.\n\nAn environment model returns one too, where the observations are those it gives and the actions those it receives, the other way round from a cognitive model.\n\nEach argument is a Tuple of Symbols, which is kept in the type, so that the names are known at compile time, and read as a property of the same name, such as attributes.parameters. A single Symbol is put in a tuple, and a Vector of them is made into one. All four must be given, and () means that the model has none of that kind.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> attributes = ModelAttributes(;\n           parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n           states = :expected_reward,\n           observations = [:reward],\n           actions = (),\n       )\nModelAttributes\nParameters: learning_rate, inverse_temperature\nStates: expected_reward\nObservations: reward\nActions: none\n\njulia> attributes.states\n(:expected_reward,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelAttributes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.ModelInput","page":"API","text":"ModelInput(observation, previous_state, previous_action, parameters, timestep_size=1,\n           rng=default_rng())\n\nEverything a model needs to run a single timestep, which apply_model receives.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as input.observation.\n\nArguments\n\nobservation: what the model observes on this timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the environment gave them, as in input.observation.reward. An observation that isn't made on this timestep is NoObservation. On the first timestep when the agent starts, every observation is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nprevious_state: the state of the model after the previous timestep. On the first timestep, this is the output of initialize_state.\nprevious_action: the actions from the previous timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the model gave them, as in input.previous_action.choice. On the first timestep, each of them is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nparameters: the cognitive parameters of the model. Will generally be a NamedTuple. \ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from if anything in the model is random, so that a simulation can be reproduced.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> observation = (; reward = 1.0);\n\njulia> state = (; expected_reward = 0.5);\n\njulia> parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.1);\n\njulia> previous_action = (; choice = BeforeFirstTimestep());\n\njulia> ModelInput(observation, state, previous_action, parameters).timestep_size\n1\n\njulia> ModelInput(observation, state, previous_action, parameters, 0.5).timestep_size\n0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelInput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.ModelOutput","page":"API","text":"ModelOutput(action_distribution, new_state=(;))\n\nThe outputs of a model for a single timestep, which apply_model returns.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as output.new_state.\n\nArguments\n\naction_distribution::NamedTuple: what the actions on this timestep are sampled from, in a NamedTuple with one entry for each action, such as (; choice = Bernoulli(0.7)). Each entry can be anything that works with rand, such as a distribution from Distributions.jl, or a Deterministic for an action that isn't random, and the sampled actions keep the names. An action that isn't taken on this timestep is NoAction, and a model without actions gives an empty NamedTuple, (;).\nnew_state=(;): the cognitive state of the model after this timestep, which is the previous_state of the next one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using Distributions: Bernoulli\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.7)), (; expected_reward = 0.5)).new_state\n(expected_reward = 0.5,)\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.7))).new_state\nNamedTuple()\n\njulia> ModelOutput((; choice = NoAction(), bet = Bernoulli(0.2))).action_distribution.choice\nNoAction()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.ModelOutput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NoAction","page":"API","text":"NoAction()\n\nStand-in for an action that isn't taken on a timestep, as one entry of the actions NamedTuple, such as (; choice = NoAction()). Before the first timestep, an action is BeforeFirstTimestep instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoAction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NoObservation","page":"API","text":"NoObservation()\n\nStand-in for an observation that isn't made on a timestep, as one entry of the observations NamedTuple, such as (; reward = NoObservation()). Before the first timestep, an observation is BeforeFirstTimestep instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NoObservation"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AbstractEnvironmentModel","page":"API","text":"AbstractEnvironmentModel\n\nSupertype for environment models, which give a cognitive model its observations.\n\nImplementation\n\nCreate a subtype and add methods of apply_model, initialize_state and attribute_names for it. The method of apply_model takes an EnvironmentInput and returns an EnvironmentOutput. An environment model can also add methods of prepare_state_for_saving, default_parameters and stopping_criterion.\n\nSee also simulate, which has an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AbstractEnvironmentModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentInput","page":"API","text":"EnvironmentInput(action, previous_state, previous_observation, parameters,\n                 timestep_size=1, rng=default_rng())\n\nEverything an environment model needs to run a single timestep.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as input.action.\n\nArguments\n\naction: the actions that the environment responds to, in a NamedTuple with the names the cognitive model gave them, as in input.action.choice. On the first timestep when the environment starts, each of them is BeforeFirstTimestep, and an action that isn't taken is NoAction.\nprevious_state: the state of the environment after the previous timestep.\nprevious_observation: the observations that were sampled on the previous timestep, in a NamedTuple with the names the environment gave them. On the first timestep, each of them is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nparameters: the parameters of the environment model.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from if anything in the environment is random, so that a simulation can be reproduced.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentInput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentOutput","page":"API","text":"EnvironmentOutput(observation_distribution, new_state=(;))\n\nThe outputs of an environment model for a single timestep, which apply_model returns.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as output.new_state.\n\nArguments\n\nobservation_distribution::NamedTuple: what the observations on this timestep are sampled from, in a NamedTuple with one entry for each observation, such as (; reward = Bernoulli(0.8)). Each entry can be anything that works with rand, such as a distribution from Distributions.jl, or a Deterministic for an observation that isn't random, as in (; reward = Deterministic(1.0)), and the sampled observations keep the names. An observation that isn't made on this timestep is NoObservation, and an environment model without observations gives an empty NamedTuple, (;).\nnew_state=(;): the state of the environment after this timestep, which is the previous_state of the next one. The observations are sampled after it is made, so it can't depend on them: the environment receives them as input.previous_observation on the next timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentOutput"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.CommonRLEnvironment","page":"API","text":"CommonRLEnvironment(rl_env; action_name)\n\nAn environment model around rl_env, an environment from CommonRLInterface.jl, which needs CommonRLInterface.jl to be loaded. action_name is the name of the cognitive model's action that act! receives, such as :arm.\n\nEnvironments from POMDPs.jl and ReinforcementLearning.jl can be used through it, with convert(CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv, x), which POMDPTools.jl defines for a POMDP or MDP and ReinforcementLearningBase.jl for its environments.\n\nrl_env itself is the state: initialize_state resets it with CommonRLInterface.jl's reset! and returns it, every timestep changes it in place with act! and passes it on as the new state, and prepare_state_for_saving copies it, with clone if it provides it and with deepcopy otherwise. A method of prepare_state_for_saving for a CommonRLEnvironment with a specific type of rl_env can save something lighter. The parameters aren't used, and neither is the random number generator, since an environment of CommonRLInterface.jl has its own randomness.\n\nEach observation is a NamedTuple (; observation, reward, terminated): what observe gives, the reward that act! returned, and whether the episode has ended. They are given as they are, each in a Deterministic, so nothing more is sampled. Before the first action, when the action is BeforeFirstTimestep, the environment is at its initial observation, so the reward is NoObservation and nothing is done with the action. The same goes for a timestep where the model takes no action, NoAction: the environment stays where it is. Its attribute_names are these three observations, the one action, no parameters and no states, since rl_env isn't a NamedTuple. Use EnvironmentStarts, so that the model observes before it acts.\n\nA simulation stops by itself when the episode ends, since the stopping_criterion of the environment is terminated of rl_env, so n_steps is the most timesteps it can take. An episode that has ended isn't started again, and an action given to it throws an error. A new call of simulate starts a new episode, and so does reset! of an Environment, since both reset rl_env through initialize_state.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing CommonRLInterface\n\n#A two-armed bandit where the first arm always pays, in episodes of two pulls\nmutable struct Bandit <: CommonRLInterface.AbstractEnv\n    pulls::Int\nend\nCommonRLInterface.reset!(rl_env::Bandit) = (rl_env.pulls = 0; nothing)\nCommonRLInterface.actions(rl_env::Bandit) = 1:2\nCommonRLInterface.observe(rl_env::Bandit) = rl_env.pulls\nCommonRLInterface.act!(rl_env::Bandit, arm) = (rl_env.pulls += 1; arm == 1 ? 1.0 : 0.0)\nCommonRLInterface.terminated(rl_env::Bandit) = rl_env.pulls == 2\n\n#A model which always pulls the first arm\nstruct PullsFirstArm <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\nCognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::PullsFirstArm, parameters) = (;)\nCognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::PullsFirstArm, input::ModelInput) = ModelOutput((; arm = Deterministic(1)))\nCognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::PullsFirstArm) = ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = (), observations = (:observation, :reward, :terminated), actions = :arm)\n\n#The simulation stops when the episode ends, before it has run all of n_steps\nobservations, actions, _, _, _ = simulate(\n    PullsFirstArm(),\n    CommonRLEnvironment(Bandit(0); action_name = :arm);\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 10,\n    cognitive_parameters = (;),\n    environment_parameters = (;),\n    save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()),\n)\n\nforeach(println, observations)\n\n# output\n\n(observation = 0, reward = NoObservation(), terminated = false)\n(observation = 1, reward = 1.0, terminated = false)\n(observation = 2, reward = 1.0, terminated = true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CommonRLEnvironment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.SaveEverything","page":"API","text":"SaveEverything()\n\nMarker for saving every attribute of a kind, used in SaveHistory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SaveEverything"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.SaveHistory","page":"API","text":"SaveHistory(; observations=(), actions=(), states=())\nSaveHistory(SaveEverything())\n\nWhich observations, actions and states a simulation saves, by name.\n\nEach argument is the names of that kind to save, in a Tuple of Symbols, a single Symbol or a Vector of them, or SaveEverything for all of them. A kind that isn't given saves nothing, so SaveHistory() saves nothing, which is the default everywhere, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. The selections are kept in the type, so that selecting costs nothing in a simulation, and are read as properties, such as save_history.states.\n\nSaving some names of a kind gives a NamedTuple with those names on each timestep, which only works when the value is a NamedTuple, and saving none gives an empty one, (;), for any value. For an environment, the states are its own, and the observations and actions are those it gave and received, which are the agent's unless a NameMap selects or renames them. simulate! adds them to the environment's history too, while simulate only uses its states.\n\nSee also CognitiveAgent, Environment and simulate, which take one.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> save_history = SaveHistory(; actions = :choice, states = (:expected_reward, :surprise))\nSaveHistory\nObservations: none\nActions: choice\nStates: expected_reward, surprise\n\njulia> save_history.states\n(:expected_reward, :surprise)\n\njulia> SaveHistory(SaveEverything()).observations\nSaveEverything()\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.SaveHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AgentHistory","page":"API","text":"AgentHistory(observations, actions, states, timestep_sizes)\n\nThe history of states, actions and observations for a CognitiveAgent, which simulate! adds to.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as history.actions. The agent's SaveHistory selects what is kept of each observation, action and state, and when it selects nothing at all, the vectors stay empty.\n\nArguments\n\nobservations::Vector{Any}: the observations, starting with one where every observation the model lists is BeforeFirstTimestep.\nactions::Vector{Any}: the actions, starting with the agent's previous action, which by default has every action as BeforeFirstTimestep().\nstates::Vector{Any}: the states of the model, starting with its initial state. Later states are those after each timestep. All of them are as returned by prepare_state_for_saving.\ntimestep_sizes::Vector{Any}: how much time each timestep covered, starting with BeforeFirstTimestep() for where the agent started, so that it has an entry for each entry of the other vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AgentHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.AgentStarts","page":"API","text":"AgentStarts()\n\nMake the agent move first on each timestep of a simulation.\n\nOn a timestep, the cognitive model updates and acts on the previous observation, and the environment then responds to its action. On the first timestep, the cognitive model receives an observation with every entry BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\nSee also EnvironmentStarts, simulate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.AgentStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.CognitiveAgent","page":"API","text":"CognitiveAgent(cognitive_model, cognitive_parameters; save_history=SaveHistory())\nCognitiveAgent(cognitive_model, cognitive_parameters, cognitive_state, previous_action;\n               save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nAn instantiated cognitive model with its parameters, current state, and previous action, which simulate! can update iteratively.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as agent.cognitive_state. Parameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if the model provides defaults for them. The state defaults to the output of initialize_state with the full parameters, and the previous action to every action being BeforeFirstTimestep. The model must have a method of attribute_names, and the names of the parameters, of the previous action, and of the state when it is a NamedTuple, must be those it lists, or an error is thrown.\n\nagent.history holds an AgentHistory with where the agent started and everything that happens to it, as selected by save_history, a SaveHistory. By default nothing is selected and the vectors in agent.history stay empty, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. Observations and actions can only be selected by name among those the model lists, since the history starts from those names.\n\nSee also Environment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.CognitiveAgent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.Environment","page":"API","text":"Environment(model, parameters; save_history=SaveHistory())\nEnvironment(model, parameters, state, previous_observation; save_history=SaveHistory())\n\nAn environment model with its parameters and current state, which simulate! can update iteratively.\n\nParameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if the environment model provides defaults for them. The state defaults to the output of initialize_state with the full parameters, and the previous observation to every observation being BeforeFirstTimestep. The environment model must have a method of attribute_names, and the names of the parameters, of the previous observation, and of the state when it is a NamedTuple, must be those it lists, or an error is thrown.\n\nenvironment.history holds an EnvironmentHistory with where the environment started and what happens to it, as selected by save_history, a SaveHistory. By default nothing is selected and the vectors in environment.history stay empty, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. Observations and actions can only be selected by name among those the environment model lists, since the history starts from those names.\n\nSee also CognitiveAgent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.Environment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentHistory","page":"API","text":"EnvironmentHistory(observations, actions, states, timestep_sizes)\n\nWhat has happened to an Environment, which simulate! adds to.\n\nEach argument is kept in a field of the same name, such as history.states. The environment's SaveHistory selects what is kept of each observation, action and state, and when it selects nothing at all, the vectors stay empty. The observations are those the environment gave and the actions those it received, which are the same as in the agent's history unless a NameMap selects or renames them.\n\nArguments\n\nobservations::Vector{Any}: the observations the environment gave, starting with its previous observation, which by default has every observation as BeforeFirstTimestep.\nactions::Vector{Any}: the actions on each timestep, starting with every action the environment lists as BeforeFirstTimestep(), since it has received none before the simulation.\nstates::Vector{Any}: the states of the environment, starting with its initial state. Later states are those after each timestep. All of them are as returned by prepare_state_for_saving.\ntimestep_sizes::Vector{Any}: how much time each timestep covered, starting with BeforeFirstTimestep() for where the environment started, so that it has an entry for each entry of the other vectors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentHistory"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentStarts","page":"API","text":"EnvironmentStarts()\n\nMake the environment move first on each timestep of a simulation.\n\nOn a timestep, the environment gives an observation in response to the previous action, and the cognitive model then updates and acts on it. On the first timestep, the environment receives an action with every entry BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\nSee also AgentStarts, simulate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.EnvironmentStarts"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.NameMap","page":"API","text":"NameMap(names...)\n\nWhich outputs of one side of a simulation the other side receives, and under which names.\n\nEach argument is :given => :received, for an output which the receiver lists under another name, or a single Symbol, for one which keeps its name. Outputs that aren't in the map aren't passed on, and each name can only be there once. The names are kept in the type, so that a map costs nothing in a simulation.\n\nsimulate and simulate! take one as observation_map, from the observations the environment model gives to those the cognitive model lists, and one as action_map, from the actions the cognitive model gives to those the environment model lists. A cognitive model and an environment which name things differently, or where one gives something the other doesn't list, can then be used together, and the map says which outputs the receiver gets: it must give exactly the names the receiver lists in attribute_names. NameMap() passes nothing on, for a receiver which lists nothing. Without a map, the two must list the same names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> NameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue)\nNameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue)\n\njulia> NameMap(:payoff => :reward, :cue => :reward)\nERROR: ArgumentError: A NameMap can only give each name once, but it gives (:reward, :reward).\n[...]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.NameMap"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.reset!","page":"API","text":"reset!(agent::CognitiveAgent)\nreset!(environment::Environment)\n\nPut agent or environment back to where a new one starts, and return it.\n\nThe state becomes the output of initialize_state with the parameters it has. The previous action of an agent has every action as BeforeFirstTimestep again, and so has every observation of the previous observation of an environment. The history is a new one which starts from there, so a history that was read before the reset, as in history = agent.history, is kept as it was. The model, the parameters and the SaveHistory stay the same.\n\nAn agent or an environment that was created with a state, a previous action or a previous observation of its own doesn't return to those. Create a new one for that.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, input::ModelInput)\n    V₋₁ = input.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + input.parameters.learning_rate * (input.observation.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, observations = :reward, actions = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = CognitiveAgent(RescorlaWagner(), (; learning_rate = 0.5); save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()))\n\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nreset!(agent)\n\nagent.cognitive_state, agent.previous_action, agent.history.states\n\n# output\n\n((expected_reward = 0.5,), (choice = BeforeFirstTimestep(),), Any[(expected_reward = 0.5,)])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.reset!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.simulate!-Tuple{CognitiveAgent, Any}","page":"API","text":"simulate!(agent::CognitiveAgent, observation; timestep_size=1, rng=default_rng())\n\nRun a single timestep of agent on observation, update it, and return the sampled action. What agent.save_history selects of the observation, the action and the state is added to agent.history, together with the size of the timestep.\n\nThe action is in a NamedTuple with the name the model gave it, as in action.choice, as for simulate. The observation must be a NamedTuple with every observation the model lists in attribute_names, or an ArgumentError is thrown.\n\nArguments\n\nagent::CognitiveAgent: the agent to update.\nobservation::NamedTuple: what the agent observes on this timestep, such as (; reward = 1.0). An observation that isn't made is NoObservation.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, input::ModelInput)\n    V₋₁ = input.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + input.parameters.learning_rate * (input.observation.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, observations = :reward, actions = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = CognitiveAgent(RescorlaWagner(), (; learning_rate = 0.5); save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()))\n\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\nsimulate!(agent, (; reward = 1.0))\n\nagent.history.states\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{Any}:\n (expected_reward = 0.5,)\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.simulate!-Tuple{CognitiveAgent, Environment}","page":"API","text":"simulate!(agent::CognitiveAgent, environment::Environment; <keyword arguments>)\n\nSimulate agent interacting with environment from where they are, update them both, and return the same as simulate: the vectors with what agent.save_history selects of the observations, actions and states, and what environment.save_history selects of the environment's states, followed by the sizes of the timesteps. What the two select is also added to agent.history and environment.history, together with the sizes of the timesteps, unless a selection is empty.\n\nThe agent's model and the environment's must fit together, as for simulate, directly or through observation_map and action_map. The agent's history holds the observations as its model received them, and the environment's history the actions as its model did.\n\nThe simulation ends before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of the environment's model is met. An environment where it is met already can't be simulated further, and throws an error until it is reset!.\n\nReading the agent's untyped fields costs a dynamic dispatch on each call, which makes the return type unknown to the compiler. The loop inside is still fully typed. Use simulate if the return type matters, or if most calls are for a single timestep.\n\nArguments\n\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: who moves first on each timestep.\nn_steps::Int: the number of timesteps to simulate, which must be at least 1. It is the most timesteps there can be when the environment's model has a stopping_criterion.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers. A value that isn't a vector, such as a single number, is the size of every timestep of this call. A vector has one size for each timestep of this call, so its length must be n_steps.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of the environment the agent receives, and under which names, as for simulate.\naction_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which actions of the agent the environment receives, and under which names, as for simulate.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RandomChoices <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RandomChoices, parameters)\n    return (;)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RandomChoices, input::ModelInput)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(0.5)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RandomChoices)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = (), observations = :n_timesteps, actions = :choice)\nend\n\nstruct TimestepCounter <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::TimestepCounter, parameters)\n    return (; n_timesteps = 0)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::TimestepCounter, input::EnvironmentInput)\n    n_timesteps = input.previous_state.n_timesteps + 1\n    return EnvironmentOutput((; n_timesteps = Deterministic(n_timesteps)), (; n_timesteps))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::TimestepCounter)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = (), states = :n_timesteps, observations = :n_timesteps, actions = :choice)\nend\n\nagent = CognitiveAgent(RandomChoices(), (;))\nenvironment = Environment(TimestepCounter(), (;))\n\nsimulate!(agent, environment; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), n_steps = 2)\nsimulate!(agent, environment; who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(), n_steps = 3)\n\nenvironment.state\n\n# output\n\n(n_timesteps = 5,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.simulate-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel, AbstractEnvironmentModel}","page":"API","text":"simulate(cognitive_model, environment; <keyword arguments>)\n\nSimulate cognitive_model interacting with environment, and return the vectors observations, actions, cognitive_states and environment_states, with one element for each timestep, followed by timestep_sizes.\n\nEach element is what happened on that timestep, and the states are those after it. save_history and save_environment_history select what is kept of each element, which is nothing by default: give SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) to keep everything. The sizes of the timesteps are returned as they were given: a single size as it is, and a vector of sizes with the sizes of the timesteps that ran.\n\nParameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if cognitive_model or environment provides defaults for them. The names of the parameters, the states, the previous action and the previous observation must be those in attribute_names, and cognitive_model and environment must list the same observations and the same actions, or an ArgumentError is thrown before the first timestep. When the two name things differently, or one gives something the other doesn't list, observation_map and action_map say which outputs of one the other receives, and under which names. The observations are then returned as cognitive_model received them, and the actions as it gave them.\n\nThe simulation ends before n_steps timesteps if stopping_criterion of environment is met, and everything returned then has the timesteps that ran.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractCognitiveModel: the cognitive model.\nenvironment::AbstractEnvironmentModel: the environment model.\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: who moves first on each timestep. It has no default, because a wrong choice shifts the model by a timestep without any error.\nn_steps::Int: the number of timesteps to simulate, which must be at least 1. It is the most timesteps there can be when environment has a stopping_criterion.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, which both cognitive_model and environment receive. A value that isn't a vector, such as a single number, is the size of every timestep. A vector has one size for each timestep, so its length must be n_steps.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of cognitive_model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\nenvironment_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of environment, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample actions and observations from, which cognitive_model and environment also receive in their inputs.\nprevious_cognitive_state=initialize_state(cognitive_model, ...): the state of cognitive_model before the first timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters. A state that cognitive_model changes in place will be changed.\nprevious_action=initial_action(cognitive_model): the action before the first timestep. By default every action is BeforeFirstTimestep, with the names from attribute_names.\nprevious_environment_state=initialize_state(environment, ...): the state of environment before the first timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters.\nprevious_observation=initial_observation(environment): the observation before the first timestep. By default every observation is BeforeFirstTimestep(), with the names from attribute_names of environment.\nsave_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which observations, actions and states of cognitive_model are saved, by name. Nothing is saved by default, and SaveHistory(SaveEverything()) saves everything. See SaveHistory.\nsave_environment_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which states of environment are saved. Only its states is used here, since the observations and actions are returned once, as save_history selects them.\nobservation_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which observations of environment are passed on to cognitive_model, and under which names, as a NameMap from the names environment gives to exactly those cognitive_model lists. Without one, the two must list the same observations, which cognitive_model receives under the same names.\naction_map::Union{Nothing, NameMap}=nothing: which actions of cognitive_model are passed on to environment, and under which names, as a NameMap from the names cognitive_model gives to exactly those environment lists. Without one, the two must list the same actions, which environment receives under the same names.\n\nSee also simulate!, which continues from where the last simulation ended.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli, BernoulliLogit\n\n#A cognitive model which learns the expected reward\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, input::ModelInput)\n    V₋₁ = input.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + input.parameters.learning_rate * (input.observation.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = BernoulliLogit(Vₜ - 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, observations = :reward, actions = :choice)\nend\n\n#An environment which takes the choice, and gives a reward with a fixed probability whatever it is\nstruct BernoulliRewards <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(environment::BernoulliRewards, parameters)\n    return (;)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(environment::BernoulliRewards, input::EnvironmentInput)\n    return EnvironmentOutput((; reward = Bernoulli(input.parameters.reward_probability)))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::BernoulliRewards)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :reward_probability, states = (), observations = :reward, actions = :choice)\nend\n\nobservations, actions, cognitive_states, environment_states, timestep_sizes = simulate(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    BernoulliRewards();\n    who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n    n_steps = 3,\n    cognitive_parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5),\n    environment_parameters = (; reward_probability = 1.0),\n    save_history = SaveHistory(SaveEverything()),\n)\n\ncognitive_states\n\n# output\n\n3-element Vector{@NamedTuple{expected_reward::Float64}}:\n (expected_reward = 0.75,)\n (expected_reward = 0.875,)\n (expected_reward = 0.9375,)\n\nExtended help\n\nThe return type is concrete as long as the types of the outputs don't change between timesteps. If they do, like an observation with an entry that is sometimes NoObservation, the vector for that output holds a Union of the types, and the simulation is a little slower.\n\nNothing is copied when outputs are saved. A model or environment which changes an object in its state in place, instead of returning a new one, must add a method of prepare_state_for_saving, or all the saved states show the final values, with no error. Observations and actions have no such function, so they must be new objects on each timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.simulate-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"simulate(cognitive_model, observation; <keyword arguments>)\n\nRun a single timestep of cognitive_model on observation, without an environment, and return the sampled action and the new state of the model.\n\nNothing is kept between calls, so pass the returned state and action on as previous_cognitive_state and previous_action to run the next timestep. simulate! on a single observation does the same for a CognitiveAgent, which keeps them. The compiler knows the types here, which it doesn't for an agent, so this is the faster of the two.\n\nThe action is in a NamedTuple with the name the model gave it, as in action.choice. The observation must be a NamedTuple with every observation the model lists in attribute_names, or an ArgumentError is thrown. Parameters that aren't given take the values in default_parameters, if cognitive_model provides defaults for them, and the names of the parameters, the state and the previous action are checked as when there is an environment.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractCognitiveModel: the cognitive model.\nobservation::NamedTuple: what the model observes on this timestep, such as (; reward = 1.0). An observation that isn't made is NoObservation.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: the parameters of cognitive_model, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\ntimestep_size=1: how much time this timestep covers.\nrng::AbstractRNG=default_rng(): the random number generator to sample from.\nprevious_cognitive_state=initialize_state(cognitive_model, ...): the state of cognitive_model before this timestep, by default its initial state from the full parameters. A state that cognitive_model changes in place will be changed.\nprevious_action=initial_action(cognitive_model): the action before this timestep. By default every action is BeforeFirstTimestep.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: Bernoulli\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, input::ModelInput)\n    V₋₁ = input.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + input.parameters.learning_rate * (input.observation.reward - V₋₁)\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Bernoulli(Vₜ)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(; parameters = :learning_rate, states = :expected_reward, observations = :reward, actions = :choice)\nend\n\ncognitive_parameters = (; learning_rate = 0.5)\n\n#The first timestep starts from the initial state\naction, cognitive_state = simulate(RescorlaWagner(), (; reward = 1.0); cognitive_parameters)\n\n#The next one continues from what the first returned\naction, cognitive_state = simulate(\n    RescorlaWagner(),\n    (; reward = 1.0);\n    cognitive_parameters,\n    previous_cognitive_state = cognitive_state,\n    previous_action = action,\n)\n\ncognitive_state\n\n# output\n\n(expected_reward = 0.875,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.simulate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.MissingAction","page":"API","text":"MissingAction()\n\nStand-in for an action that was taken on a timestep but isn't in the data, as one entry of the actions NamedTuple, such as (; choice = MissingAction()). Fitting imputes it by sampling it from the model's action distribution on that timestep, or passes it on as NoAction when it is told not to impute. NoAction itself is different: it says that the model took no action on that timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.MissingAction"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.MissingObservation","page":"API","text":"MissingObservation()\n\nStand-in for an observation that was made on a timestep but isn't in the data, as one entry of the observations NamedTuple, such as (; reward = MissingObservation()). Fitting imputes it from what an environment model gives for that observation on the timestep: as a latent variable with that distribution, or as the value of a Deterministic. NoObservation is different: it says that nothing was observed on that timestep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.MissingObservation"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_single_session","page":"API","text":"turing_single_session(cognitive_model, cognitive_parameters, observations, actions;\n                      <keyword arguments>)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model of one session: cognitive_model with cognitive_parameters goes through the timesteps of the data, and the recorded actions are scored against the action distributions it gives. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nThe model is a building block for a Turing.jl model which samples the parameters, as a submodel with its own prefix: to_submodel(prefix(turing_single_session(...), @varname(session)), false). It returns what save_history selects of the observations, the actions and the states of the session, as a NamedTuple of vectors with one element for each timestep, which returned(model, chain) gives for each sample. Its timestep_sizes has the size of each timestep when anything is selected, and is empty otherwise. Nothing is selected by default.\n\nOn each timestep, turing_apply_model of cognitive_model runs as a submodel with the prefix timestep[t], so a model which samples within a timestep has its own variables for each timestep, such as timestep[3].noise. The recorded actions are scored together at the end, in one product_distribution over every action and timestep, which is several times faster than scoring each timestep on its own.\n\nArguments\n\ncognitive_model::AbstractCognitiveModel: the cognitive model.\ncognitive_parameters::NamedTuple: its parameters, usually the random variables of the model around this one. Those it doesn't have take the values in default_parameters, if the model provides defaults for them.\nobservations::NamedTuple: the observations in the data, with one vector for each name and one entry for each timestep, such as (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0]). The data must have every observation the model lists in attribute_names, and can have more. An observation that wasn't recorded is MissingObservation, which environment fills in.\nactions::NamedTuple: the actions in the data, in the same form, with exactly the actions the model lists. An action that was taken but not recorded is MissingAction, and is imputed unless impute_missing_actions is false. An action that wasn't taken is NoAction, and isn't scored.\nwho_starts::Union{AgentStarts, EnvironmentStarts}: whether the model acts on the observation of the same timestep, EnvironmentStarts(), or on the observation of the timestep before, AgentStarts(), as in simulate. It has no default, because a wrong choice shifts the model by a timestep without any error.\nenvironment::Union{Nothing, AbstractEnvironmentModel}=nothing: an environment model which fills in the observations that are MissingObservation(). When it is given, it runs on every timestep as a submodel with the prefix timestep[t].environment, and what it gives for an observation is only used where the data has none: the missing observation is then imputed from it, as a latent variable with that distribution, or as the value itself if it is a Deterministic. Its turing_apply_model must sample with ~ if anything else in it is random. It must list the same observations and the same actions as cognitive_model, since a session takes no maps of names. Without it, the data can't have missing observations.\nenvironment_parameters::NamedTuple=(;): the parameters of environment, with its default values for those that aren't given, if it provides any.\ntimestep_sizes=1: how much time each timestep covers, as in simulate.\nimpute_missing_actions::Bool=true: whether an action that is MissingAction() is imputed, as a latent variable. When it is false, nothing is sampled for it, and it is passed on as NoAction() instead: cognitive_model receives NoAction() as that previous action on the next timestep, and environment as the action it responds to.\nsave_history::SaveHistory=SaveHistory(): which observations, actions and states are returned, by name. The returned actions and observations are those the model received, with the imputed and filled in values.\ncatch_invalid_parameters::Bool=false: whether an InvalidParametersError from the model gives the parameters a log density of -Inf, so that a sampler rejects them, instead of stopping the sampler. It is off by default because the try block it needs can't be differentiated with Mooncake, and a compiled ReverseDiff tape doesn't see the error, so the parameters get a finite log density with no warning. It works with ForwardDiff and Enzyme.\nprevious_cognitive_state, previous_action, previous_observation, previous_environment_state: where the session starts, with the same defaults as in simulate.\n\nAn imputed action is a latent variable named after its timestep and action, such as timestep[3].choice.imputed, with the model's action distribution on that timestep as its distribution. An observation imputed from a distribution is one in the same way, under the environment's prefix, such as timestep[3].environment.reward.imputed. The sampler must handle its type: a continuous action can be sampled with NUTS, a discrete one needs a sampler for discrete variables. Any latent variable in a session, imputed or from a model which samples within a timestep, makes evaluating the model much slower in the current version of DynamicPPL.jl.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\n#The model to fit, a Rescorla-Wagner model with a softmax choice\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n    return (; expected_reward = 0.5)\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, input::ModelInput)\n    V₋₁ = input.previous_state.expected_reward\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + input.parameters.learning_rate * (input.observation.reward - V₋₁)\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(input.parameters.inverse_temperature * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n        states = :expected_reward,\n        observations = :reward,\n        actions = :choice,\n    )\nend\n\n#The data of one session, where the third choice wasn't recorded\nobservations = (; reward = [1.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0])\nactions = (; choice = [true, false, MissingAction(), true])\n\n#Priors on the parameters, and the session as a submodel\n@model function fit_one_session(observations, actions)\n    learning_rate ~ Beta(2, 2)\n    inverse_temperature ~ LogNormal(0, 1)\n    session ~ to_submodel(\n        prefix(\n            turing_single_session(\n                RescorlaWagner(),\n                (; learning_rate, inverse_temperature),\n                observations,\n                actions;\n                who_starts = EnvironmentStarts(),\n                save_history = SaveHistory(; states = :expected_reward),\n            ),\n            @varname(session),\n        ),\n        false,\n    )\n    return session\nend\n\nchain = sample(fit_one_session(observations, actions), NUTS(), 1000)\n\n#The expected rewards on each timestep, for each sample\ntrajectories = returned(fit_one_session(observations, actions), chain)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_single_session"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.apply_model-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"apply_model(model::AbstractCognitiveModel, input::ModelInput)::ModelOutput\n\nRun a single timestep of model. Usually this involves updating cognitive states and returning an object from which the action is sampled.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. Anything random should be sampled from input.rng, so that a simulation can be reproduced.  If states are mutated in place, a preparestatefor_saving method must be added. For use within PPLs, there may be special constraints - see the documentation for the specific PPL for details.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: BernoulliLogit\n\nstruct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::RescorlaWagner, input::ModelInput)\n\n    ## Unpack input ##\n    #Reward observed on this timestep\n    r = input.observation.reward\n    #Expected reward from the previous state\n    V₋₁ = input.previous_state.expected_reward\n    #Learning rate parameter\n    α = input.parameters.learning_rate\n    #Inverse temperature parameter\n    β = input.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    ## Update state ##\n    #Update expected reward using the Rescorla-Wagner rule\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + α * (r - V₋₁)\n\n    ## Compute action probabilities ##\n    #Softmax function, instantiated as a Bernoulli logit distribution\n    Pₜ = BernoulliLogit(β * (Vₜ - 0.5))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\nparameters = (; learning_rate = 0.1, inverse_temperature = 2.0)\ninput = ModelInput((; reward = 1.0), (; expected_reward = 0.5), (; choice = NoAction()), parameters)\n\nCognitiveModeling.apply_model(RescorlaWagner(), input).new_state\n\n# output\n\n(expected_reward = 0.55,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.apply_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_state-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"initialize_state(model::AbstractCognitiveModel, parameters)\n\nCreate the initial state that model has before its first timestep, which can depend on parameters.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A model without a state returns an empty NamedTuple, (;).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::RescorlaWagner, parameters)\n           return (; expected_reward = parameters.initial_value)\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(RescorlaWagner(), (; initial_value = 0.5))\n(expected_reward = 0.5,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.attribute_names-Tuple{AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"API","text":"attribute_names(model::AbstractCognitiveModel)::ModelAttributes\n\nReturn the names of the parameters, states, observations and actions of model, in a ModelAttributes.\n\nThe names define the model, so they must match what the model uses. The parameters are the names that apply_model and initialize_state read from the parameters. The states are the names in the state that initialize_state returns and in new_state, which apply_model reads from input.previous_state. The observations are the names that apply_model reads from input.observation, and the actions are the names in action_distribution, which are also those in input.previous_action. Nothing checks that they match while the model runs.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A model without one kind of attribute gives () for it. simulate and CognitiveAgent use the action names for the action before the first timestep, and check the parameters, the previous action and a NamedTuple state against the names. A simulation with an environment also checks that the two fit together: they must list the same observations and the same actions, unless a NameMap says which of them are passed on, and under which names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct RescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::RescorlaWagner)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = (:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature),\n               states = :expected_reward,\n               observations = :reward,\n               actions = :choice,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(RescorlaWagner()).parameters\n(:learning_rate, :inverse_temperature)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.attribute_names"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.apply_model-Tuple{AbstractEnvironmentModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"apply_model(environment, input::EnvironmentInput)::EnvironmentOutput\n\nRun a single timestep of environment: update its state, and return what the next observation is sampled from.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery environment model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. A simulation samples the observations itself, from the observation_distribution of the EnvironmentOutput that the method returns, so an observation that isn't random is wrapped in a Deterministic. Anything else that is random, such as a change of the state, should be sampled from input.rng, so that a simulation can be reproduced.\n\nSee also simulate, which has an example.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.apply_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.initialize_state-Tuple{AbstractEnvironmentModel, Any}","page":"API","text":"initialize_state(environment::AbstractEnvironmentModel, parameters)\n\nCreate the state that environment has before its first timestep, which can depend on parameters.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery environment model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. An environment model without a state returns an empty NamedTuple, (;).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.initialize_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.attribute_names-Tuple{AbstractEnvironmentModel}","page":"API","text":"attribute_names(environment::AbstractEnvironmentModel)::ModelAttributes\n\nReturn the names of the parameters, states, observations and actions of environment, in a ModelAttributes.\n\nThe observations are the names in the observation_distribution that apply_model returns, which the cognitive model receives, and the actions are the names it reads from input.action, which the cognitive model gives. The parameters and the states are as for a cognitive model.\n\nImplementation\n\nEvery environment model must add a method of this function, which throws an error otherwise. An environment model without one kind of attribute gives () for it, and so does one whose state isn't a NamedTuple. simulate and Environment use the observation names for the observation before the first timestep, and check the parameters, the previous observation and a NamedTuple state against the names. A simulation also checks that the environment model and the cognitive model fit together: they must list the same observations and the same actions, unless a NameMap says which of them are passed on, and under which names.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct BernoulliRewards <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::BernoulliRewards)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = :reward_probability,\n               states = (),\n               observations = :reward,\n               actions = :choice,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(BernoulliRewards()).observations\n(:reward,)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.attribute_names"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving","page":"API","text":"prepare_state_for_saving(model::AbstractCognitiveModel, state)\nprepare_state_for_saving(environment::AbstractEnvironmentModel, state)\n\nReturn what a simulation saves of state, the state of a cognitive model or of an environment model, as it is about to save it. The state that the next timestep starts from isn't affected.\n\nImplementation\n\nA cognitive model or an environment model can add a method of this function. The default returns state as it is, so nothing is copied. One which changes an object in its state in place must add a method which copies it, or all the saved states show the final values, with no error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct LearnsInPlace <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(model::LearnsInPlace, state)\n           return (; values = copy(state.values))\n       end;\n\njulia> state = (; values = [0.5, 0.5]);\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving(LearnsInPlace(), state).values === state.values\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.prepare_state_for_saving"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.default_parameters","page":"API","text":"default_parameters(model::AbstractCognitiveModel)\ndefault_parameters(environment::AbstractEnvironmentModel)\n\nReturn the default parameter values of a cognitive model or of an environment model, such as (; learning_rate = 0.1), which CognitiveAgent, Environment and simulate use for the parameters they aren't given.\n\nImplementation\n\nA cognitive model or an environment model can add a method of this function. The default is an empty NamedTuple, (;), so there are no default values.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.default_parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_model","page":"API","text":"turing_apply_model(model::AbstractCognitiveModel, input::ModelInput)\nturing_apply_model(environment::AbstractEnvironmentModel, input::EnvironmentInput)\n\nCreate a Turing.jl model for a single timestep of model, to be used as a submodel, which returns a ModelOutput, or of environment, which returns an EnvironmentOutput. This needs Turing.jl to be loaded.\n\nImplementation\n\nA model can add a method of this function. The default calls apply_model. A model which samples within a timestep needs to add a method, however; or if any other changes are desired within Turing. It must sample with ~ instead of from input.rng. Otherwise the model has a different log density each time it is evaluated, with no error. The same holds for an environment model, which fitting uses to fill in observations that are MissingObservation in the data: what it samples itself, such as a change of its state, must be sampled with ~, while fitting samples the observations from what it returns.\n\nExamples\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Turing\n\nstruct NoisyRescorlaWagner <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\nTuring.@model function CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_model(\n        model::NoisyRescorlaWagner,\n        input::ModelInput,\n    )\n\n    ## Unpack input ##\n    #Reward observed on this timestep\n    r = input.observation.reward\n    #Expected reward from the previous state\n    V₋₁ = input.previous_state.expected_reward\n    #Learning rate parameter\n    α = input.parameters.learning_rate\n\n    ## Update state ##\n    #Sample the noise in the update\n    ϵ ~ Normal(0, input.parameters.noise)\n    #Update expected reward using the Rescorla-Wagner rule\n    Vₜ = V₋₁ + α * (r - V₋₁) + ϵ\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = BernoulliLogit(Vₜ - 0.5)), (; expected_reward = Vₜ))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.turing_apply_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion","page":"API","text":"stopping_criterion(environment::AbstractEnvironmentModel, state, parameters)\n\nReturn whether environment has ended, with state as its state after a timestep and its parameters, so that a simulation stops there instead of running all of n_steps.\n\nsimulate and simulate! check it before each timestep, with the state that the timestep before left. When it returns true, no further timestep runs, and what is returned and added to the histories has the timesteps that ran. When the environment starts, the cognitive model has then made its last observation and acted on it, and the environment doesn't receive that action. An environment whose criterion is already met before the first timestep can't be simulated, and throws an error: a new simulation starts from the initial state, and reset! puts an Environment back there.\n\nImplementation\n\nAn environment model can add a method of this function. The default returns false, so a simulation runs all of its timesteps.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct Maze <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion(environment::Maze, state, parameters)\n           return state.position == parameters.goal\n       end;\n\njulia> CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion(Maze(), (; position = 4), (; goal = 4))\ntrue\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"CognitiveModeling.stopping_criterion"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.show-Tuple{IO, MIME{Symbol(\"text/plain\")}, AbstractCognitiveModel}","page":"API","text":"show(io::IO, ::MIME\"text/plain\", model::AbstractCognitiveModel)\n\nPrint a summary of model and the names of its attributes.\n\nThis is what the REPL and notebooks show. Everywhere else, such as in print or inside a vector, a model is printed on one line by Julia's default show(io, model).\n\nThe attributes show as not specified if model has no method of attribute_names, and a kind of attribute shows as none if the model has no names of that kind.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct ExampleModel <: AbstractCognitiveModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::ExampleModel)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = (:learning_rate, :noise),\n               states = :expected_reward,\n               observations = :reward,\n               actions = (),\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> ExampleModel()\n-- Cognitive model --\nType: ExampleModel\nParameters: learning_rate, noise\nStates: expected_reward\nObservations: reward\nActions: none\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.show-Tuple{IO, MIME{Symbol(\"text/plain\")}, AbstractEnvironmentModel}","page":"API","text":"show(io::IO, ::MIME\"text/plain\", environment::AbstractEnvironmentModel)\n\nPrint a summary of environment and the names of its attributes, as for a cognitive model.\n\nThe attributes show as not specified if environment has no method of attribute_names, and a kind of attribute shows as none if the environment model has no names of that kind.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> struct ExampleEnvironment <: AbstractEnvironmentModel end\n\njulia> function CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(environment::ExampleEnvironment)\n           return ModelAttributes(;\n               parameters = :reward_probability,\n               states = (),\n               observations = :reward,\n               actions = :choice,\n           )\n       end;\n\njulia> ExampleEnvironment()\n-- Environment model --\nType: ExampleEnvironment\nParameters: reward_probability\nStates: none\nObservations: reward\nActions: choice\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.show"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/compiling_with_reactant/#Compiling-simulations-with-Reactant.jl","page":"Compiling simulations with Reactant.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to compile a simulation with Reactant.jl, and how to take its gradient with Enzyme.jl. Until then, this is what to know before trying.\n\nShort simulations can be compiled with Reactant.jl, if the action distributions work with traced numbers, which those from Distributions.jl don't. Compile a function that takes a Reactant.ReactantRNG() as a positional argument and passes it on as rng, because @jit doesn't trace keyword arguments, and the default generator repeats its numbers on every run. Compile times grow quickly with n_steps, so this is for a few hundred timesteps at most.\n\nThe page Automatic differentiation and Reactant lists what is tested, and the pitfalls a model can run into.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Compiling simulations with Reactant.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_actr_models/#Models-from-ACTRModels.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from ACTRModels.jl as cognitive models","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use ACTRModels.jl, a package for building ACT-R models, inside a cognitive model.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Models from ACTRModels.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting started","text":"Placeholder tutorial. Each file in docs/literate/ is a plain Julia script that becomes a page like this one when the documentation is built.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_sequential_sampling_models/#Models-from-SequentialSamplingModels.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from SequentialSamplingModels.jl as cognitive models","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use the sequential sampling models of SequentialSamplingModels.jl, such as the drift diffusion model, as what a cognitive model samples its actions from, so that it gives both a choice and a response time.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Models from SequentialSamplingModels.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#Models-from-POMDPs.jl-as-cognitive-models","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"POMDPs.jl describes a task as a problem object, and has solvers which give a policy for it, and updaters which keep a belief about the hidden state. A cognitive model can keep such a belief as its state, update it with their updater, and choose from the values their policy gives each action.\n\nThis page shows such a cognitive model. It doesn't simulate or fit it yet, and its code isn't run when the documentation is built, to keep the documentation's environment small. It was run by hand with POMDPs.jl 1.0, POMDPTools.jl 1.1 and QMDP.jl 0.1.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\nusing Distributions: DiscreteNonParametric\nusing LogExpFunctions: softmax\nusing POMDPs: POMDPs\nusing POMDPTools: DiscreteUpdater, actionvalues\nusing QMDP: QMDPSolver","title":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#A-Bayesian-observer-with-a-solved-policy","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"The cognitive model holds the problem, a policy for it and a belief updater. The problem is solved once, when the cognitive model is created, so nothing is solved on a timestep.\n\nstruct BayesianObserver{Tpomdp, Tpomdp_policy, Tpomdp_updater} <: AbstractCognitiveModel\n    pomdp::Tpomdp\n    pomdp_policy::Tpomdp_policy\n    pomdp_updater::Tpomdp_updater\nend\n\nfunction BayesianObserver(pomdp)\n    return BayesianObserver(pomdp, POMDPs.solve(QMDPSolver(), pomdp), DiscreteUpdater(pomdp))\nend\n\nIts state is the belief, which starts as the problem's distribution over initial states.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.initialize_state(model::BayesianObserver, parameters)\n    return (; belief = POMDPs.initialize_belief(model.pomdp_updater, POMDPs.initialstate(model.pomdp)))\nend\n\nOn each timestep, the belief is updated from the previous action and the observation that followed it, and the action probabilities are a softmax over the values the policy gives each action under that belief, with the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl. On the first timestep there is no previous action, which AbstractNoValue covers, and the belief is left as it is.\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.apply_model(model::BayesianObserver, input::ModelInput)\n\n    # Unpack input ##\n    belief = input.previous_state.belief\n    previous_choice = input.previous_action.choice\n    β = input.parameters.inverse_temperature\n\n    # Update state ##\n    #Update the belief from the previous action and what was observed after it\n    if !(previous_choice isa AbstractNoValue)\n        belief = POMDPs.update(model.pomdp_updater, belief, previous_choice, input.observation.observation)\n    end\n\n    # Compute action probabilities ##\n    #The value the policy gives each action under the belief\n    Qₜ = actionvalues(model.pomdp_policy, belief)\n    #Softmax over the action values, as a distribution over the problem's own actions\n    Pₜ = DiscreteNonParametric(collect(POMDPs.actions(model.pomdp)), softmax(β .* Qₜ))\n\n    return ModelOutput((; choice = Pₜ), (; belief))\nend\n\nfunction CognitiveModeling.attribute_names(model::BayesianObserver)\n    return ModelAttributes(;\n        parameters = :inverse_temperature,\n        states = :belief,\n        observations = :observation,\n        actions = :choice,\n    )\nend\n\nThe updater returns a new belief on each timestep, so the saved states need no copying.\n\nA problem from POMDPModels.jl gives a cognitive model for its task, such as BayesianObserver(TigerPOMDP()). The problem can be used as the environment too, through CommonRLEnvironment. That environment also gives reward and terminated, which this cognitive model doesn't list, so a NameMap must select the observation for it: observation_map = NameMap(:observation).\n\nThe learners in TabularTDLearning.jl can't be used this way. They are solvers which run every episode themselves, so there is no single timestep to call.","title":"A Bayesian observer with a solved policy"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_pomdps/#Fitting","page":"Models from POMDPs.jl as cognitive models","text":"This page doesn't fit the cognitive model yet. When it is fitted with a sampler that uses gradients, use Enzyme.jl in reverse mode with runtime activity as the automatic differentiation backend, which for Turing.jl is AutoEnzyme(; mode = set_runtime_activity(Reverse), function_annotation = Const). It gave the right gradient of the log density of a session for this cognitive model, when checked against finite differences with Enzyme.jl 0.13. So did ForwardDiff.jl and ReverseDiff.jl, while Mooncake.jl and Enzyme's forward mode threw an error.\n\nUse the softmax from LogExpFunctions.jl, as above, and not one written by hand. Written in one line, a softmax got a wrong gradient for this cognitive model from Enzyme's reverse mode, with no error, which the page Automatic differentiation and Reactant describes under its pitfalls.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Fitting"},{"category":"section","location":"#CognitiveModeling.jl","page":"Home","text":"A Julia package for computational modeling of cognition and behaviour. It is under development.","title":"CognitiveModeling.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/interfacing_with_common_rl/#Interfacing-with-CommonRLInterface.jl","page":"Interfacing with CommonRLInterface.jl","text":"Placeholder tutorial. It will show how to use an environment from CommonRLInterface.jl as the environment of a cognitive model, which also covers the environments of POMDPs.jl and ReinforcementLearning.jl, since they convert to it.\n\nusing CognitiveModeling\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Interfacing with CommonRLInterface.jl"}]
}
